       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/45c30a3354c38331cc42d11962474554?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FJW-1.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Jared Whiklo",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a4c12a753d0071341edb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>Questions:<br/>\nCan we track a list of the objects that have been reindexed?<br/>\nDo the objects come out of ocfl-java in a consistent order?</p>\n\n<p>Each object is reindexed in it’s own database transaction which is then committed, so we probably use the database as the record of already indexed children. </p>\n\n<p>Direct/Indirect container relationships are generated <em>after</em> all the other objects are indexed.</p>\n\n<p>So with some (Jared’s suggestion here) new flag to resume indexing, the server would re-run the IndexBuilder but skip any object with an entry in the ocflIndex or containmentIndex.</p>\n\n<p>Alternative suggestion is to write a file on reindex of all processed objects. On Fedora startup if that file exists then continue reindexing. Once reindexing is complete, remove the file.</p>",
     "created": "05/Oct/23 1:00 PM",
     "id": "22889",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/13810/comment/22889",
